  "title": "Remembering a carry",
  "body": " Remembering a carry  Begin with . Write the numbers one above the other, lining up their rightmost digits. In the units column, seven and eight make fifteen. Write the below the line and carry the above the next column. In that column, eight and six and the carried one make fifteen again. Write another , and carry another . Finally, five and four and the carried one make ten. Write in the hundreds place and put the last in the thousands place. The answer is . Notice where the information went. The worker did not have to remember the carried ones until they were needed. They were written above the next column. The answer digits already found stayed below the line. The two numbers being added stayed in place. The paper was doing part of the remembering.  It helps to use graph paper, with one digit in each square. Keep a finger above the column being worked. From there, the first number is one square down, the second two squares down, and the answer three squares down. This arrangement stays the same as the finger travels left. In the following pictures the gray square marks that finger’s place. Each picture shows the work just before its column is done.  Beside each picture is the card for what the worker sees there. Read the first card, imagine making its marks, then look at the second picture: it is the page those directions leave behind.   Two graph-paper snapshots for 587 plus 468. Units card: blank, 7, 8. Write 5 three squares below the focus, write 1 one square left, then shift focus left. Tens card: 1, 8, 6. Follow the same directions. The second snapshot shows the units answer 5 and a carried 1.     The next two snapshots for 587 plus 468. Hundreds card: 1, 5, 4. Write 0 three squares below the focus, write 1 one square left, then shift focus left. Thousands card: 1, blank, blank. Write 1 three squares below the focus. The addition is finished: 1055.    The answer grows from right to left while the original numbers stay where they were. The last step is different: the finger reaches a column with no digits in either number. There is still a carried one to put below the line. Once that is written, there is nothing left to add. A card has supplied both an arithmetic fact and the movements needed to use it.  Suppose our first sheet had ended just to the left of the five in . The hundreds card would now ask us to put a carried one off the edge. Before carrying it out, set another sheet against that edge and align the rows. The missing column is now available:   A new sheet is aligned against the left edge of the first sheet. The rows continue across the join, providing space for the carried 1 and the thousands digit of 1055 beneath 587 plus 468.    The new squares continue the old rows; the worker makes the same one-square move across the join. We can do this at any edge. Eight adjoining sheets can surround the starting sheet, one at each side and one at each corner. The picture separates their edges so we can see the individual sheets; on the desk their grids join together.   Nine sheets of graph paper arranged in a three-by-three square. The shaded central starting sheet is surrounded by eight adjoining sheets, continuing the graph paper at every side and corner.    If the calculation reaches the outside of this larger patch, add more sheets there. This is how an unlimited supply of ordinary graph paper becomes a grid that can extend as far as needed in any direction. After any finite number of steps, the worker has used only finitely many sheets.  "
