  "title": "Putting the small facts on the sheet",
  "body": " Putting the small facts on the sheet  Our four cards finish this particular addition. Suppose the next problem has a nine above a four in its units column. None of those cards covers what the worker sees. We could supply another card saying to write three and carry one. But another problem could expose another missing pair. To leave the worker with a sheet that handles any two numbers, we supply every pair of digits, both with and without an incoming carry. Someone preparing the cards does these small additions once; the worker can then follow them whenever those marks appear.  Each number square can contain one of ten digits, or it can be blank. That makes eleven possibilities for each square, hence pairs. Allowing for a carry or no carry gives cases. Two of these have blanks in both number squares; they tell the worker to finish, as we will see below. The other cases handle a column containing at least one digit. Each case has its own directions on the sheet.  The original numbers might be enormous, but they do not create a new kind of column. A person adding numbers a million digits long still uses this same finite collection of cases, repeated from right to left.  We can arrange these facts in two lookup tables: one for a column without a carry, and one for a column with a carry. In either table, the upper mark selects a row and the lower mark selects a column. Their meeting place gives the answer digit and the next carry. Blank has its own row and column. The appendix prints both tables. Here are the three entries used in our addition:         upper  lower  incoming carry  write  carry left    7  8  blank  5  1    8  6  1  5  1    5  4  1  0  1    The worker finds the entry by comparing marks.  How does the worker know when to stop? Look at the two number squares in the next column. If both are blank, there are no more digits to add. Write the remaining carry, if there is one, and stop. In , this last carry supplies the in .  If just one square is blank, there is still a column to do. For , the units give five. In the tens column there is a one above a blank, with a blank in the carry square. Its table entry says: write one below the line, leave a blank in the next carry square, and move on: Move left once more. Both number squares are blank and no carry remains, so the answer is finished.  "
