  "title": "The metatheorem’s exact scope",
  "body": " The metatheorem’s exact scope  Here first-order has the meaning used in Henkin’s construction: there is and every range over the individual objects supplied by the model. Our variables do not directly range over every possible collection of those objects or over all possible interpretations. When the objects themselves include sets, quantifiers may range over those sets as objects, with the range still supplied by the model.  Let be a consistent classical first-order theory with computably enumerable certificates. Assume an effective sentence compiler , correct in the intended finite-record interpretation, and assume that every actual finite halting computation yields effectively a -proof of its output sentence. The recursion theorem then produces that searches for certificates of and reacts by producing .  Fix the compiler and the certificate enumeration before constructing . The task supplied to Kleene’s construction receives a program text , enumerates certified conclusions, and looks for one in the specified form . The word is recoverable from that syntactic form by the fixed coding convention. If a matching conclusion appears, the task writes that word and halts. Kleene supplies a complete program that performs this task with its own text in place of ; there is no extra axiom asserting that the program knows its text.  If any such certificate existed, the first one encountered would produce an actual finite run. Finite-trace verification would supply the opposite theorem, contradicting consistency. Thus for each standard . By the deduction theorem, is consistent. Consistency is our assumption about ; the proofs required within concern the particular finite executions.  The excluding certificate, if one existed, would occupy a finite place in the enumeration. Reaching and responding to it would be an ordinary finite execution, including the reconstruction of the program’s description. The finite-trace hypothesis certifies that particular execution; it does not require to prove that the search always succeeds or that the division programs always terminate.  To see the consistency conclusion directly in our calculus, suppose proved . Discharge every use of the extra assumption by the negation rule. The result would be a -proof of , which the preceding argument excludes. This proves the conclusion for each prescribed ordinary finite word; it does not assert the simultaneous consistency of incompatible outputs.  The program of Chapter  yields a true unprovable nonhalting assertion. The positive halting sentence may remain provable in a consistent theory. The broader result of essential incompleteness follows from AS’s interpretation strength: every consistent computably enumerable extension of AS leaves some sentence undecided.  "
